      "role": "supplementary",
      "technique": "Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC)",
      "why": "DSC identifies the glass transition temperature (Tg) or melting/crystallization transitions (Tm/Tc) of your shape memory polymer, which is essential for selecting the exact programming, shape-fixing, and recovery temperatures before mechanical testing. It is destructive to the small specimen pan sample and does not measure force.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Standard or hermetic sample pans",
        "Cooling accessory if transitions occur below room temperature"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [
    {
      "technique": "Dedicated axial Dynamic Mechanical Analyzer (DMA)",
      "why_useful": "A dedicated linear DMA with controlled-force and iso-strain modes is the standard instrument for executing automated, full shape-memory cycles (heating, programming, cooling, unloading, and constrained thermal recovery) on small polymer strips."
    }
  ],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "To measure the actuation force of a shape memory polymer, hold the programmed sample at a fixed strain (isostrain condition) and record the force generated as you apply the activation stimulus. For smaller samples generating low-to-moderate forces during thermal activation, the Discovery HR20 rheometer (EQ21) is recommended because its Environmental Test Chamber is already confirmed; for larger forces, use the Instron 5967 universal testing system (EQ15) after confirming an environmental chamber or activation setup. You can also use the DSC (EQ18) beforehand to pinpoint the polymer's thermal transition temperatures."
}

